/// A built-in collection member named in a case other than its documented one.
///
/// This is the one member of the family where the two processors already
/// disagree, and it is the only defect here that a differential test could have
/// caught.  The runtime compares built-in member names with a case-insensitive
/// compare, so compiled code accepts these; the interpreter's method tables
/// compare exactly, so it rejects them.  The interpreter is normative, so its
/// answer is the language's answer today -- and it is the half that will have
/// to change.
///
/// Fails with: Undefined property 'contains'.  Compiled with --compile --test,
/// this same file reports [ PASS ] and exits 0.
///
test 'A built-in collection member resolves through any spelling';
begin
    var Values := [1, 2, 3];

    AssertTrue  (Values.contains (2));
    AssertEqual (3, Values.length);
end
